---
import { WebpComponent } from "../components/webp.component";
import Layout from "../layout/Layout.astro";
const title = 'Convert HEIC to WebP Online — Quality and Resize Controls';
const description = 'Convert HEIC to WebP locally. Adjust quality, resize the longest edge and compare output dimensions and file sizes before publishing your photos.';
const ogImage = "/og-image/heic-to-webp.png";
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} ogImage={ogImage} ogTitle={title}>
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4">
    <h1 class="text-center font-bold text-4xl mt-12 mb-5">Convert HEIC to WebP</h1>
    <p class="text-center text-zinc-700 mb-8">Make a WebP copy for a website or app that accepts it. Each selected HEIC becomes a separate WebP image; Download All packages the images in a ZIP.</p>
    <WebpComponent client:visible />
    <section class="mt-12 mb-10 prose max-w-none" aria-labelledby="conversion-steps">
      <h2 id="conversion-steps">How to convert HEIC to WebP</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Use <strong>Choose HEIC photos</strong> to select files from your device.</li>
        <li>Choose your output settings, then select Convert to WebP.</li>
        <li>Use a named download link to save an image, or choose Download All as ZIP for an archive of the separate results. The cards show dimensions and file sizes.</li>
        <li>Check the downloaded result before sharing it. Choose <strong>Convert More</strong> to start another batch.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Selected photo bytes are processed locally in your browser rather than uploaded to a conversion server. The website still loads its scripts and other web resources. No account or conversion fee is required.</p>
      <p>For alternatives and troubleshooting, read the <a href="/blog/heic-to-webp-ultimate-guide/">HEIC to WebP guide</a> and <a href="/how-it-works/">how conversion works</a>.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="prose max-w-none mb-10" aria-labelledby="conversion-limits">
      <h2 id="conversion-limits">What to check before using the result</h2>
      <p>Choose a lossy WebP quality setting from 10 to 100 (default 80), with optional longest-edge resizing. Smaller photos are not enlarged. There is no lossless-mode switch or exact target-file-size control. WebP output is not guaranteed to be smaller than your HEIC or an existing website image.</p>
      <p>Large photos or batches can need substantial memory. If conversion fails, try a single complete HEIC file, then a smaller batch. Support can vary with the HEIC variant, browser and available device memory.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="prose max-w-none mb-16" aria-labelledby="format-choice">
      <h2 id="format-choice">When to choose WebP</h2>
      <p>Inspect color, orientation and fine details at the size you intend to display. Compare actual file sizes, check that your publishing system accepts WebP, and resize with your editor or image pipeline if needed. Keep the original HEIC.</p>
      <p>Compare <a href="/blog/heic-jpg-png-pdf-which-format/">JPG, PNG, WebP and PDF</a>, or use the <a href="/blog/heic-photo-upload-not-supported/">photo upload checklist</a> if a form still rejects your file.</p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
